# Template for the document root's .htaccess — copy it into place on deploy:
#
#     cp public/.htaccess.example public/.htaccess
#
# The live .htaccess is deliberately NOT tracked (see .gitignore). Hosting
# panels such as cPanel own that file and rewrite it — for example the
# "# php -- BEGIN cPanel-generated handler" block that pins the PHP version.
# A tracked copy would collide with the server on every deploy, and pulling
# over it would silently drop that block, leaving the app on the wrong PHP.
#
# So: copy this once, then let the server keep its own copy. If you re-copy it
# later, re-add any panel-generated blocks afterwards.
#
# THIS directory is the document root. Point the vhost here and nowhere else:
# .env, config/, src/, tests/, vendor/, the templates and the noah CLI all sit
# one level above it and are therefore not addressable at all. That is the
# difference between this file and the one it replaced, which was an allow-list
# guarding a document root that held the whole repository (E7.1, #139).
#
# Which means there is no refusal here for them to fail at. Everything below
# this line is a page or an asset, and the only rules are the ones that route
# and cache.

# HTTPS, and never anything else. A 301 so the browser remembers, above every
# other rule so there is one canonical scheme for the whole site.
#
# Three guards, and each one is a way this rule goes wrong:
#
# %{HTTPS} off is the plain question, and on this origin it is the true one --
# Cloudflare talks to it over TLS (the session cookie comes back `secure`, which
# it only does when PHP sees HTTPS), so for ordinary traffic this rule never
# fires. What it catches is somebody reaching the origin directly over port 80.
#
# X-Forwarded-Proto is the loop guard, and the reason to write it now rather
# than after. If the distribution in front is ever switched to talk to the
# origin over plain HTTP -- Cloudflare calls that Flexible -- then %{HTTPS} is
# off for *every* request, this rule redirects to https://, the distribution
# answers that over TLS and asks the origin again over HTTP, and the site is an
# infinite redirect for everyone. The header says what the browser actually
# used, so with it there is no loop to have.
#
# And localhost, because development is http://fh-trip-builder.localhost:8888
# and there is no certificate there to redirect to. HTTP_HOST carries the port
# when it is not the default, hence the optional group.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
    RewriteEngine On
    RewriteCond %{HTTPS} off
    RewriteCond %{HTTP:X-Forwarded-Proto} !https
    RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} !(^|\.)localhost(:[0-9]+)?$ [NC]
    RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} !^127\.0\.0\.1(:[0-9]+)?$
    RewriteRule ^ https://%{HTTP_HOST}%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]
</IfModule>

# Except dotfiles. Nothing here starts with a dot today; this is for the ones
# that arrive without being decided on -- .htaccess itself, a .DS_Store copied
# up with an image, an editor's .swp.
#
# .well-known is the exception to the exception, and it is not optional: that
# is where cPanel AutoSSL and Let's Encrypt write their challenge files, in
# this directory because it is the document root. Refusing it renews nothing
# and the certificate expires 90 days later with no warning attached.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
    RewriteEngine On
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} !^/\.well-known/
    RewriteRule (^|/)\. - [F]
</IfModule>

# Bearer tokens, which do not arrive on their own.
#
# Apache hands `Authorization` to the CGI/FPM process only when told to, so on
# this deployment `$_SERVER` held no AUTH key at all and `getallheaders()`
# answered `Accept`, `User-Agent`, `Host` and nothing else -- on a request that
# carried a token. Every authenticated call to /api/* answered 401, for every
# client, for as long as the endpoints have existed (E28, #207).
#
# A rewrite and not `CGIPassAuth On`, which says the same thing in one line and
# is the better answer where it exists: it is an Apache 2.4.13+ core directive,
# so on anything older the whole file is a 500 and the site is down. This needs
# only mod_rewrite, which every other rule here already needs.
#
# The value lands in `REDIRECT_HTTP_AUTHORIZATION` once the front-controller
# rewrite below has run, because an internal redirect prefixes what mod_rewrite
# set -- Request::headersFromServer() reads both spellings.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
    RewriteEngine On
    RewriteCond %{HTTP:Authorization} .
    RewriteRule ^ - [E=HTTP_AUTHORIZATION:%{HTTP:Authorization}]
</IfModule>

# Front-controller routing: send every request that doesn't map to a real file
# or directory through index.php, so clean URLs like /search/ and
# /api/airports/autofill reach the router (index.php reads the path from
# REQUEST_URI). Static assets still serve directly via the -f/-d checks --
# css/, js/, fonts/ and img/, which is everything else in here.
# Apache only; nginx does this in its vhost with try_files ... /index.php.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
    RewriteEngine On
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d
    RewriteRule ^ index.php [QSA,L]
</IfModule>

# The fonts are served from this app rather than from Google (see the #FONTS
# section of css/main.css). Two things they need from the server:
#
# The media type, because Apache only learned font/woff2 in 2.4.26 and an older
# host would serve them as application/octet-stream. Browsers still accept that
# when the stylesheet declares format("woff2"), but the correct type is what
# lets compression and caching rules match on it.
<IfModule mod_mime.c>
    AddType font/woff2 .woff2
</IfModule>

# And a long cache. A font file's contents never change -- a new version is a
# new filename -- so there is nothing to revalidate. `immutable` tells the
# browser not to even ask on a reload.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
    <FilesMatch "\.woff2$">
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
    </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# Already compressed: woff2 is Brotli-compressed internally, so running it
# through gzip again costs CPU and adds bytes.
<IfModule mod_deflate.c>
    SetEnvIfNoCase Request_URI "\.woff2$" no-gzip dont-vary
</IfModule>
